-- Migration: Question generation — AUD batch 42 (30 questions)
-- Date: 2026-02-23
-- Topic: Misstatements and Control Deficiencies
-- Difficulty: 9E / 15M / 6H
-- Bloom's: L1:8 / L2:9 / L3:8 / L4:5

BEGIN;

INSERT INTO questions (section_id, topic, stem, choices, correct_index, explanation, difficulty, cognitive_level)
VALUES

-- Q1: easy / L1 — Tolerable misstatement purpose
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'What is the primary purpose of setting tolerable misstatement for an audit?', '["To establish the overall materiality level for the financial statements as a whole", "To determine the appropriate fee structure for the audit engagement", "To limit the acceptable misstatement in a class of transactions or account balance so that the aggregate does not exceed overall materiality", "To set the threshold for communicating control deficiencies to management"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 320.09, tolerable misstatement is the application of performance materiality to a particular sampling procedure, limiting acceptable misstatement in individual account balances or transaction classes. It is set below overall materiality so that the aggregate of uncorrected and undetected misstatements does not exceed materiality for the financial statements as a whole. Unlike overall materiality (choice A), tolerable misstatement operates at the account or class level, not the financial statement level.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q2: medium / L2 — Communication timeline for significant deficiencies
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Redwood Financial Group, the engagement partner identifies a significant deficiency in the revenue recognition process on October 15, 2025. The audit report date is December 1, 2025. Under AU-C 265, the auditor must communicate significant deficiencies to those charged with governance:', '["Verbally within 30 days of the date of discovery during fieldwork", "In writing no later than 60 days following the audit report release date", "In writing before the audit report date to allow management time to respond", "Only after obtaining management''s written response to the deficiency"]'::jsonb, 1, 'AU-C 265.13 requires the auditor to communicate significant deficiencies and material weaknesses in writing to those charged with governance no later than 60 days following the report release date. While timely communication is encouraged, the standard sets the 60-day deadline as the outer limit. Choice C is incorrect because the standard does not require communication before the audit report date, though earlier communication is considered best practice.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q3: medium / L2 — Clearly trivial threshold application
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'While auditing Granite Peak Enterprises, the auditor discovers a $200 misstatement in office supplies expense. Overall materiality is set at $500,000 and the clearly trivial threshold is $2,500. Under AU-C 450, the auditor should:', '["Not accumulate the misstatement because it falls below the clearly trivial threshold", "Add the misstatement to the summary of uncorrected misstatements for governance review", "Request management to correct the misstatement before issuing the audit report", "Evaluate the misstatement for potential fraud indicators regardless of the dollar amount"]'::jsonb, 0, 'AU-C 450.05 states that the auditor accumulates identified misstatements during the audit other than those that are clearly trivial. AU-C 450.A2 explains that clearly trivial is not another expression for not material—amounts clearly trivial are those the auditor designates as clearly inconsequential. Since $200 is below the $2,500 threshold, it need not be accumulated. Choice B is incorrect because only misstatements exceeding the clearly trivial threshold are accumulated on the summary.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q4: easy / L2 — Management acknowledgment of uncorrected misstatements
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'At the completion of the audit of Clearwater Technologies, the auditor presents a summary of uncorrected misstatements totaling $45,000 to management. Materiality is $200,000. Under AU-C 450, the auditor should request that management:', '["Sign a waiver accepting full legal responsibility for all identified misstatements", "Adjust the financial statements regardless of the aggregate amount involved", "Disclose all uncorrected misstatements individually in the notes to the financial statements", "Correct the misstatements or acknowledge in the representation letter that the effects are immaterial"]'::jsonb, 3, 'AU-C 450.08 requires the auditor to communicate accumulated misstatements to management and request correction. If management does not correct all misstatements, AU-C 580.16 requires a written representation that management believes the effects of uncorrected misstatements are immaterial, individually and in the aggregate. Choice B is incorrect because the auditor requests correction but cannot require it when the misstatements are immaterial.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q5: hard / L4 — Pattern of small misstatements suggesting fraud
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Pacific Ridge Industries, the auditor identifies three revenue misstatements, all overstating revenue by recording sales before the performance obligation is satisfied. Each misstatement is individually below the clearly trivial threshold. The pattern suggests intentional manipulation by the sales department. Under AU-C 240 and AU-C 450, the auditor should:', '["Disregard the misstatements entirely because each is individually below the clearly trivial threshold", "Evaluate the misstatements collectively and consider whether they indicate a risk of material misstatement due to fraud", "Immediately withdraw from the engagement and report the findings to regulators", "Accumulate the misstatements as routine errors since each is below the clearly trivial amount"]'::jsonb, 1, 'AU-C 240.35 requires the auditor to evaluate whether identified misstatements may be indicative of fraud, regardless of dollar amount. AU-C 450.A3 notes that clearly trivial amounts may still warrant consideration when circumstances suggest intentional misstatement. The pattern of directionally consistent revenue overstatements requires reassessment of fraud risk. Choice A is incorrect because the fraud indicators override the clearly trivial threshold—the auditor cannot disregard misstatements suggesting intentional manipulation.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q6: medium / L2 — Representation letter content for uncorrected misstatements
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'While completing the audit of Thornton Manufacturing, the auditor requests management include representations about uncorrected misstatements in the management representation letter. The summary shows $35,000 in aggregate uncorrected misstatements against a $300,000 materiality level. Under AU-C 580, the representation letter should include:', '["A detailed explanation of each individual misstatement and why management chose not to correct it", "A statement confirming that management has investigated all potential misstatement sources and found no others", "Management''s belief that the effects of uncorrected misstatements are immaterial, individually and in aggregate, to the financial statements as a whole", "An acknowledgment that the auditor assumes responsibility for any undiscovered misstatements"]'::jsonb, 2, 'AU-C 580.16 requires the auditor to request a written representation from management that it believes the effects of uncorrected misstatements are immaterial, individually and in the aggregate, to the financial statements as a whole. A summary of such items is included or attached to the representation letter. Choice A is incorrect because the standard requires a belief statement about immateriality, not detailed explanations for each individual misstatement.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q7: medium / L2 — Reassessing materiality as misstatements accumulate
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Bayshore Hospitality Group, overall materiality is $250,000. Accumulated uncorrected misstatements reach $180,000. The auditor discovers an additional $40,000 misstatement in prepaid expenses. Under AU-C 450, the auditor''s most appropriate response is to:', '["Reassess whether the aggregate of uncorrected misstatements is material and consider whether overall materiality for the audit remains appropriate", "Immediately issue a qualified opinion because accumulated misstatements exceed 75% of materiality", "Accept the additional misstatement without further action since it does not individually exceed materiality", "Request that management correct only the newest $40,000 misstatement to bring the aggregate below a comfortable margin"]'::jsonb, 0, 'AU-C 450.11 requires the auditor to determine whether uncorrected misstatements are material, individually or in the aggregate. When the aggregate approaches materiality ($220,000 of $250,000), the auditor must reassess the overall audit strategy and whether materiality remains appropriate under AU-C 320.12. Choice B is incorrect because there is no automatic threshold at 75%—the auditor exercises professional judgment rather than applying a mechanical percentage.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q8: hard / L4 — Prior-period misstatement effects on current year
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Atlas Manufacturing had $120,000 in uncorrected misstatements from the prior year audit that affected the opening balance of inventory. Current-year materiality is $400,000. The engagement team determines that the prior-period misstatements have a $95,000 effect on the current year''s cost of goods sold. Under AU-C 450, the auditor should:', '["Exclude the prior-period misstatements entirely since they were evaluated and accepted in the prior audit", "Require management to restate the prior-year financial statements before issuing the current-year opinion", "Treat the entire $120,000 as a current-year misstatement since it affects opening account balances", "Include the $95,000 current-year effect when evaluating the aggregate of uncorrected misstatements for the current period"]'::jsonb, 3, 'AU-C 450.06 requires the auditor to consider the effect of uncorrected misstatements from prior periods on current-period financial statements. The relevant amount is the $95,000 effect on current-year cost of goods sold, not the full $120,000 balance sheet carryforward. This amount must be included in the current-year aggregate evaluation. Choice A is incorrect because prior-period misstatements cannot be ignored when they have a quantifiable effect on current-period income statement amounts.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q9: hard / L4 — Fraud-related misstatement audit implications
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Sterling Electronics'' auditor identifies a $75,000 inventory overstatement caused by the warehouse manager intentionally altering cycle count sheets to conceal inventory shortages. Materiality is $500,000. Although below materiality, under AU-C 240 and AU-C 450, the auditor must:', '["Simply accumulate the misstatement as a routine error since it is below materiality", "Report the misstatement to law enforcement before taking any other audit actions", "Reconsider the risk assessment, evaluate the implications for other aspects of the audit, and consider the effect on the auditor''s report", "Accept management''s offer to terminate the warehouse manager as sufficient remediation"]'::jsonb, 2, 'AU-C 240.36-.37 requires that when the auditor identifies a misstatement resulting from fraud, the auditor must reconsider the assessed risks of material misstatement due to fraud, evaluate implications for other aspects of the audit, and consider effects on the auditor''s report—regardless of the dollar amount. Choice A is incorrect because a fraud-related misstatement demands broader audit reconsideration beyond simple accumulation, even when the amount is below materiality.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q10: medium / L2 — Pervasive vs material distinction for opinion type
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Golden Crest Retailers, uncorrected misstatements aggregate $600,000 against materiality of $400,000. The misstatements are concentrated in the inventory account and do not affect cash flows or equity. Under AU-C 705, when determining the type of modified opinion, the auditor assesses whether the misstatements are:', '["Automatically requiring an adverse opinion because the aggregate exceeds overall materiality", "Material but not pervasive, warranting a qualified opinion, or material and pervasive, warranting an adverse opinion", "Subject to management correction within 30 days before any opinion modification is required", "Reported only as an emphasis-of-matter paragraph without modifying the audit opinion"]'::jsonb, 1, 'AU-C 705.08-.09 requires the auditor to distinguish between material misstatements that are not pervasive (qualified opinion) and those that are material and pervasive (adverse opinion). Pervasive means the effects are not confined to specific elements but affect a substantial proportion of the financial statements. Since these misstatements are concentrated in inventory only, they may not be pervasive. Choice A is incorrect because exceeding materiality alone does not automatically require an adverse opinion—pervasiveness must also be evaluated.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q11: medium / L2 — IT segregation of duties control deficiency
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Pinnacle Data Solutions, the auditor discovers that the IT administrator has both the ability to initiate and approve user access changes in the financial reporting system, with no independent review of access logs. Under AU-C 265, this lack of segregation of duties in the IT environment is most appropriately classified as:', '["A control deficiency that requires evaluation for severity as a significant deficiency or material weakness", "An acceptable practice for small IT departments that does not constitute a control deficiency", "A finding that must be reported to external regulatory authorities before communicating to governance", "A deficiency only if unauthorized transactions have actually been processed through the system"]'::jsonb, 0, 'AU-C 265.07 defines a control deficiency as existing when the design or operation of a control does not allow management or employees to prevent or detect misstatements on a timely basis. Lack of segregation of duties in IT access management is a design deficiency requiring severity evaluation. Choice D is incorrect because a control deficiency exists based on the design or operation of the control, not solely on whether an actual misstatement has occurred.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q12: easy / L1 — Accumulation requirement under AU-C 450
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'What does AU-C 450 require the auditor to do with identified misstatements during the audit?', '["Report only those misstatements that are individually material to those charged with governance", "Correct all identified misstatements regardless of their dollar amount or nature", "Accumulate only misstatements that individually exceed performance materiality for the engagement", "Accumulate all identified misstatements during the audit other than those that are clearly trivial"]'::jsonb, 3, 'AU-C 450.05 requires the auditor to accumulate all misstatements identified during the audit, other than those that are clearly trivial. This accumulation enables the auditor to evaluate whether uncorrected misstatements are material, individually or in the aggregate. Choice A is incorrect because the auditor must accumulate all misstatements above the clearly trivial threshold, not only those that are individually material.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q13: hard / L4 — Deficiency severity despite no detected misstatements
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Evergreen Healthcare processes $50 million in annual patient billing through its revenue cycle system. The auditor identifies that no independent reconciliation exists between the billing system and the general ledger. Testing reveals no actual misstatements in the current period. Under AU-C 265, the auditor evaluates this control deficiency considering that:', '["The absence of actual misstatements means the deficiency cannot be classified as a significant deficiency", "The deficiency should be classified as inconsequential because substantive testing found no errors", "The magnitude of the potential misstatement and the volume of transactions processed by the control are more relevant than whether actual misstatements occurred", "Only deficiencies that result in detected misstatements can be elevated to material weakness status"]'::jsonb, 2, 'AU-C 265.A8-.A12 explains that the severity of a control deficiency depends on the magnitude of the potential misstatement and the likelihood that it could occur—not on whether a misstatement has actually been detected. With $50 million flowing through an unreconciled system, the potential misstatement is significant. Choice A is incorrect because the absence of detected misstatements does not preclude classification as a significant deficiency or material weakness.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q14: medium / L2 — Judgmental misstatement from accounting estimate
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'While auditing Cascade Energy Corp., the auditor concludes that a reasonable range for the asset retirement obligation is $2.1 million to $2.8 million, with a point estimate of $2.3 million. Management has recorded $2.1 million. Materiality is $1 million. Under AU-C 540 and AU-C 450, the $200,000 difference between the auditor''s point estimate and management''s recorded amount represents:', '["An error requiring immediate correction before the auditor can issue an unmodified opinion", "A judgmental misstatement that should be accumulated with other uncorrected misstatements", "A projected misstatement because it is derived from an estimation of the total population", "An acceptable variance that need not be accumulated because management''s recorded amount falls within the reasonable range"]'::jsonb, 1, 'AU-C 540.18 and AU-C 450.A5 classify the difference between the auditor''s point estimate and management''s recorded amount as a judgmental misstatement. Even though management''s $2.1 million falls within the reasonable range, the $200,000 difference must be accumulated with other uncorrected misstatements for aggregate evaluation. Choice D is incorrect because falling within the reasonable range does not exempt the difference from accumulation as a judgmental misstatement.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q15: easy / L1 — Iron curtain vs rollover approaches
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'What are the two approaches commonly used to evaluate the effect of prior-period uncorrected misstatements on current-period financial statements?', '["The iron curtain approach and the rollover approach", "The top-down approach and the bottom-up approach", "The quantitative approach and the qualitative approach", "The gross approach and the net approach"]'::jsonb, 0, 'SEC Staff Accounting Bulletin (SAB) No. 108 and AU-C 450.A16 reference two approaches for evaluating uncorrected misstatements: the iron curtain approach (focuses on the balance sheet, correcting the cumulative effect) and the rollover approach (focuses on the income statement, considering only the current-year effect). Choice C is incorrect because quantitative and qualitative are factors in the evaluation, not the names of the two recognized approaches.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q16: medium / L3 — Remediation testing after control fix
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the interim audit of Highland Manufacturing in August 2025, the engagement team identified a material weakness in the bank reconciliation process. Management implemented new reconciliation controls in September 2025. For the December 31, 2025, year-end audit, the engagement team should:', '["Remove the material weakness from the communication letter since management took corrective action", "Rely on management''s assertion that the new controls are effective without performing additional testing", "Report the material weakness as remediated based solely on the improved design of the new reconciliation controls", "Test the operating effectiveness of the new controls for a sufficient period before concluding the material weakness has been remediated"]'::jsonb, 3, 'AU-C 265.A17 and AU-C 330.14 require the auditor to obtain sufficient evidence that redesigned controls operate effectively for a sufficient period before concluding that a previously identified material weakness has been remediated. Design evaluation alone is insufficient—the auditor must test operating effectiveness. Choice A is incorrect because the material weakness cannot be removed from the communication simply because management implemented corrective action; effective operation must be verified through testing.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q17: medium / L3 — Qualitative factors overriding quantitative immateriality
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Meridian Financial Services, the auditor identifies a $30,000 understatement of compensation expense. Overall materiality is $500,000. However, the misstatement causes the company to meet a debt covenant ratio that it would otherwise violate. Under AU-C 450, the auditor''s evaluation should:', '["Treat the misstatement as immaterial because it represents only 6% of overall materiality", "Consider qualitative factors, including the effect on debt covenant compliance, which may make the misstatement material despite its small dollar amount", "Automatically qualify the audit opinion because the misstatement affects a contractual obligation", "Require management to disclose the misstatement in the notes regardless of the auditor''s materiality assessment"]'::jsonb, 1, 'AU-C 450.11 and .A20-.A21 require the auditor to consider both quantitative and qualitative factors when evaluating uncorrected misstatements. A misstatement that converts a covenant violation into compliance is qualitatively significant because it may influence user decisions. Choice A is incorrect because quantitative analysis alone is insufficient—qualitative factors such as effects on contractual obligations, trends, or regulatory compliance can render an otherwise immaterial misstatement material.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q18: easy / L1 — Written representation requirement for uncorrected misstatements
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'What written representation must the auditor obtain from management regarding uncorrected misstatements?', '["That all identified misstatements have been corrected in the final financial statements", "That management accepts full legal liability for any uncorrected misstatements discovered", "That management believes the effects of uncorrected misstatements are immaterial, individually and in the aggregate, to the financial statements as a whole", "That no misstatements exist in the financial statements beyond those the auditor identified"]'::jsonb, 2, 'AU-C 580.16 requires the auditor to request a written representation that management believes the effects of uncorrected misstatements are immaterial, individually and in the aggregate, to the financial statements as a whole. A summary of such items must be included in or attached to the letter. Choice A is incorrect because the representation addresses uncorrected misstatements management chose not to fix, not a claim that all misstatements were corrected.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q19: medium / L3 — Pattern of journal entries suggesting fraud
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'While auditing Noble Construction, the engagement team discovers that three journal entries posted by the controller each quarter-end reclassify expenses to a prepaid account, overstating income by $45,000 annually. The entries lack supporting documentation and materiality is $200,000. The auditor should:', '["Treat the pattern as a potential fraud indicator, expand testing of the controller''s journal entries, and communicate the findings to those charged with governance", "Treat the entries as routine reclassifications since the aggregate amount is below materiality", "Accept the entries if the controller provides a reasonable verbal explanation for the reclassifications", "Include the $45,000 as an uncorrected misstatement without conducting further investigation into the cause"]'::jsonb, 0, 'AU-C 240.32-.33 requires the auditor to evaluate whether misstatements may be indicative of fraud. A recurring pattern of unsupported entries that consistently overstate income is a fraud indicator requiring expanded procedures and communication with governance under AU-C 240.40. Choice B is incorrect because the below-materiality amount does not eliminate the obligation to investigate patterns suggesting intentional manipulation of the financial statements.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q20: easy / L1 — Strongest indicator of material weakness
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Which of the following is the strongest indicator that a control deficiency constitutes a material weakness?', '["Identification of a single immaterial error in a routine cash disbursement transaction", "A minor delay in completing a month-end bank reconciliation within the required timeframe", "An employee lacking formal training in a non-financial administrative reporting process", "Restatement of previously issued financial statements to correct a material misstatement"]'::jsonb, 3, 'AU-C 265.A14 identifies restatement of previously issued financial statements to correct a material misstatement as a strong indicator of a material weakness. A restatement demonstrates that existing controls failed to prevent or detect a material misstatement. Choice A is incorrect because a single immaterial error in a routine transaction is typically a control deficiency of lesser severity and does not, by itself, indicate a material weakness.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q21: easy / L1 — Documentation requirements for accumulated misstatements
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'What must the auditor include in audit documentation regarding misstatements accumulated during the audit?', '["Only the misstatements that management ultimately refused to correct at engagement completion", "The amount below which misstatements are clearly trivial, all accumulated misstatements, and whether each has been corrected", "Only the aggregate total of all misstatements without details for individual items", "Misstatements that individually exceed performance materiality for the audit engagement only"]'::jsonb, 1, 'AU-C 450.14 requires the auditor to document the clearly trivial threshold, all misstatements accumulated during the audit, whether they have been corrected, and the auditor''s conclusion on whether uncorrected misstatements are material. Choice A is incorrect because documentation must include all accumulated misstatements—both corrected and uncorrected—not only those management refused to correct.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q22: hard / L4 — Aggregating deficiencies across multiple locations
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Vertex Retail operates 15 store locations. The audit engagement team identifies that 8 locations lack independent review of cash register overrides and 6 locations have no daily cash reconciliation procedure. No single location''s deficiency individually rises to a significant deficiency. Under AU-C 265, the auditor should:', '["Evaluate whether the combination of individually insignificant deficiencies across locations constitutes a significant deficiency or material weakness in the aggregate", "Report each deficiency separately to each location''s individual store manager without performing any aggregation", "Classify each deficiency as inconsequential because no individual location poses a material financial risk", "Evaluate the deficiencies in aggregate only if the identical control is missing at all 15 locations"]'::jsonb, 0, 'AU-C 265.A11 requires the auditor to consider whether a combination of control deficiencies that individually are not significant deficiencies may in combination represent a significant deficiency or material weakness. Deficiencies affecting multiple locations that relate to the same financial statement assertion should be aggregated for evaluation. Choice C is incorrect because the absence of individual significance does not preclude the combination of deficiencies from reaching significant deficiency or material weakness severity.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q23: medium / L3 — Corrected misstatements still indicate control deficiency
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Copperfield Industries, the engagement team identifies 12 misstatements in accounts payable, all of which management promptly corrects. The misstatements resulted from the same control failure—inadequate three-way matching of purchase orders, receiving reports, and vendor invoices. Under AU-C 265 and AU-C 450, the auditor should:', '["Disregard the corrected misstatements entirely since they no longer affect the financial statements", "Report the 12 misstatements as uncorrected because the underlying control deficiency has not been remediated", "Evaluate the underlying cause of the corrected misstatements as a potential control deficiency even though the financial statement effect has been eliminated", "Require management to implement new three-way matching controls before the auditor can issue the report"]'::jsonb, 2, 'AU-C 265.08 requires evaluation of control deficiencies regardless of whether the resulting misstatements were corrected. AU-C 450.07 states that the auditor should consider the nature of corrected misstatements and the circumstances of their occurrence. Twelve misstatements from one control failure indicate a systemic weakness. Choice A is incorrect because correction of the financial statement effect does not eliminate the underlying control deficiency that needs evaluation and communication.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q24: medium / L3 — Compensating control effect on deficiency severity
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'While auditing Summit Logistics, the engagement team identifies that the purchasing manager can both approve and process purchase orders without secondary approval. However, the CFO reviews a detailed monthly report of all purchase orders exceeding $1,000 and investigates budget variances. Under AU-C 265, the auditor should:', '["Classify the deficiency as a material weakness regardless of any compensating controls in place", "Ignore the compensating monthly review because it is performed monthly rather than at the transaction level", "Eliminate the deficiency from the governance communication letter because the compensating control fully mitigates the risk", "Consider whether the CFO''s compensating review reduces the severity of the segregation-of-duties deficiency when classifying its significance"]'::jsonb, 3, 'AU-C 265.A9 indicates that the auditor should consider compensating controls when evaluating the severity of an identified control deficiency. A detective control such as the CFO''s monthly review may reduce the likelihood of a misstatement going undetected, thereby lowering the severity classification. Choice C is incorrect because a compensating control reduces severity but does not eliminate the underlying deficiency—the deficiency still exists and must be communicated.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q25: medium / L2 — Interim communication of control deficiencies
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During interim fieldwork at Ridgeline Corp. in September 2025, the engagement team identifies a significant deficiency in the payroll process. The year-end audit is scheduled for February 2026. Under AU-C 265, the auditor:', '["Should communicate the significant deficiency to those charged with governance on a timely basis, which may occur before the audit report date", "Must wait until the year-end audit is complete to communicate any identified control deficiencies", "Should communicate only verbally during interim since written communication is required only at year-end completion", "Is prohibited from communicating the deficiency until confirming whether it persists at December 31 year-end"]'::jsonb, 0, 'AU-C 265.12 states that the auditor should communicate significant deficiencies and material weaknesses to those charged with governance on a timely basis. AU-C 265.A18 notes that timely communication may occur during the audit rather than after. Waiting until year-end is not required. Choice B is incorrect because the standard encourages earlier communication so that management and governance can take prompt remedial action.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q26: medium / L3 — Omitted disclosure as a misstatement
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Northwind Pharmaceuticals, the auditor determines that a required disclosure about a pending $8 million lawsuit with a probable unfavorable outcome was omitted from the notes to the financial statements. Under AU-C 450, the auditor should:', '["Classify the omission as a control deficiency rather than a misstatement since no recorded dollar amount is incorrect", "Treat the omitted disclosure as a misstatement and evaluate its effect on the financial statements, including whether a modified opinion is warranted", "Require only that management add the disclosure without evaluating the omission''s effect on the audit opinion", "Issue an unmodified opinion with an emphasis-of-matter paragraph referencing the omitted disclosure"]'::jsonb, 1, 'AU-C 450.02 defines misstatements broadly to include omissions of required disclosures, not just errors in recorded amounts. An omitted disclosure about a probable $8 million lawsuit is a misstatement that must be evaluated for materiality and its effect on the auditor''s opinion. Choice A is incorrect because misstatements under AU-C 450 encompass omitted or inadequate disclosures, not solely numerical errors in account balances.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q27: easy / L1 — Significant deficiency vs control deficiency distinction
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 265, what distinguishes a significant deficiency from a control deficiency?', '["A significant deficiency always results in a material misstatement of the financial statements", "A control deficiency must be communicated in writing while a significant deficiency requires only verbal notification", "A significant deficiency is severe enough to merit the attention of those charged with governance, though it is less severe than a material weakness", "A significant deficiency can only be identified through substantive testing while a control deficiency is identified through tests of controls"]'::jsonb, 2, 'AU-C 265.07 defines a significant deficiency as a deficiency, or combination of deficiencies, in internal control that is less severe than a material weakness yet important enough to merit attention by those charged with governance. It represents an elevated severity level between a control deficiency and a material weakness. Choice A is incorrect because a significant deficiency indicates a reasonable possibility of more-than-inconsequential misstatement, not that a material misstatement has necessarily occurred.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q28: hard / L3 — Near-miss aggregate approaching materiality
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Harbor View Financial, accumulated uncorrected misstatements total $380,000 against a materiality level of $400,000. The auditor identifies a likely additional misstatement in the loan loss reserve estimated between $10,000 and $50,000. Under AU-C 450, the auditor''s most appropriate course of action is to:', '["Issue an unmodified opinion because the confirmed aggregate of $380,000 does not exceed the $400,000 materiality threshold", "Automatically issue a qualified opinion because the confirmed aggregate is within 5% of overall materiality", "Reduce materiality to $350,000 to create a larger buffer between the aggregate and the revised threshold", "Request that management correct sufficient misstatements to reduce the aggregate well below materiality, and if management refuses, consider the effect on the auditor''s opinion"]'::jsonb, 3, 'AU-C 450.11 and .A22 require the auditor to evaluate the aggregate of uncorrected misstatements and consider requesting management to correct them. When the aggregate approaches or could exceed materiality with the additional estimated misstatement ($380,000 + up to $50,000 = $430,000), the auditor should seek correction. Choice A is incorrect because the additional likely misstatement could push the aggregate past $400,000, and the auditor cannot simply ignore the risk of exceeding materiality.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q29: easy / L1 — Clearly trivial definition
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 450, how are clearly trivial misstatements defined?', '["Misstatements that are clearly inconsequential, whether taken individually or in the aggregate, and whether judged by size, nature, or circumstances", "Misstatements that fall below 1% of net income regardless of any qualitative factors", "Misstatements identified during interim testing that are not confirmed at the year-end audit date", "Misstatements that management has agreed in writing to correct before the report release date"]'::jsonb, 0, 'AU-C 450.A2 defines clearly trivial as amounts the auditor designates as clearly inconsequential, whether judged by size, nature, or circumstances. This is not another expression for not material—it is a substantially lower threshold. Choice B is incorrect because the clearly trivial threshold is based on the auditor''s professional judgment considering size, nature, and circumstances, not a fixed percentage of any benchmark.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q30: medium / L3 — Control environment weakness cascading to lower-level controls
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Pinnacle Resources, the engagement team determines that the board of directors does not actively oversee financial reporting and has not established an audit committee. Multiple lower-level control deficiencies exist in accounts payable, revenue, and payroll. Under AU-C 265, the auditor should consider whether the weak control environment:', '["Is irrelevant to the classification of lower-level deficiencies since entity-level and process-level controls are evaluated independently of each other", "Increases the severity of the individual lower-level control deficiencies because the entity-level control environment permeates and affects all other controls", "Automatically renders all identified deficiencies as material weaknesses regardless of their individual severity assessment", "Should be communicated only to the executive management team and not to those charged with governance"]'::jsonb, 1, 'AU-C 265.A11-.A12 explains that entity-level controls, including the control environment and governance oversight, affect the operating effectiveness of other controls. A weak control environment can increase the severity of lower-level deficiencies because compensating oversight is absent. Choice A is incorrect because entity-level and process-level controls are not evaluated independently—weaknesses at the entity level can elevate the severity of process-level deficiencies throughout the organization.', 'medium', 3)
;

COMMIT;
